          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following BEST explains why early diagnosis of inflammatory arthrit</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following BEST explains why early diagnosis of inflammatory arthritis is critical?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anti-inflammatory drugs can cure the underlying immune process if given early</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. NSAIDs and other anti-inflammatory drugs control symptoms but do not modify the disease course or prevent joint erosion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inflammatory arthritis always resolves spontaneously within six months of onset</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Inflammatory arthritis is a chronic condition that does not resolve spontaneously and, untreated, leads to progressive joint destruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Disease-modifying drugs can prevent joint destruction, but only if started before significant damage occurs</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. DMARDs target the underlying immune-mediated process and can prevent irreversible joint destruction — even a six-month delay between symptom onset and treatment worsens radiographic outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Joint replacement is the only effective treatment once symptoms begin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. While joint replacement may be needed in advanced disease, DMARDs started early can prevent reaching that stage entirely.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review this concept: unlike osteoarthritis, inflammatory arthritis is immune-driven and can be halted with disease-modifying drugs — but the window narrows with every month of delay."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Early DMARD initiation is the single most important factor in preventing irreversible joint damage in inflammatory arthritis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
