          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the pathogenesis of rheumatoid arthritis, pannus tissue is destructive becaus</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the pathogenesis of rheumatoid arthritis, pannus tissue is destructive because it:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deposits calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the joint space</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Calcium pyrophosphate deposition causes pseudogout, an entirely different crystal arthropathy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proliferates over cartilage and releases enzymes that erode underlying bone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Pannus is a layer of proliferating inflammatory tissue that spreads across articular cartilage, releasing collagenases that break down collagen and activating osteoclasts that dissolve bone — producing the characteristic erosions seen on X-ray.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blocks synovial fluid production, causing the joint to dry out</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. In RA, the inflamed synovium actually produces excess fluid (effusion), not reduced synovial fluid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Converts the synovial membrane into fibrous scar tissue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Pannus involves active inflammatory proliferation with enzymatic tissue destruction, not simple fibrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Revisit this: pannus is the bridge between immune activation and structural joint damage — it proliferates over cartilage and activates bone-resorbing cells."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Understanding pannus-driven destruction explains why DMARDs — which suppress the immune cascade behind pannus — can halt disease progression."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
