            "to": 801.10725
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with swelling of the distal interphalangeal joints and a diff</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with swelling of the distal interphalangeal joints and a diffusely swollen 'sausage-shaped' ring finger. These findings MOST strongly suggest:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rheumatoid arthritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Rheumatoid arthritis characteristically spares the DIP joints and does not produce dactylitis — it targets the MCP, PIP, and wrist joints symmetrically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ankylosing spondylitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Ankylosing spondylitis predominantly affects the sacroiliac joints and spine, not the peripheral DIP joints, and dactylitis is not its hallmark.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteoarthritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. While osteoarthritis can affect DIP joints (producing Heberden's nodes), the swelling is bony and non-inflammatory, and dactylitis does not occur.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Psoriatic arthritis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. DIP joint arthritis combined with dactylitis (sausage digit) is virtually pathognomonic for psoriatic arthritis — these findings are absent in RA and AS.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember this anchor: DIP joint arthritis and dactylitis (sausage digit) are essentially unique to psoriatic arthritis — they never occur in rheumatoid arthritis and are absent in ankylosing spondylitis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. DIP involvement plus dactylitis is the bedside anchor that separates psoriatic arthritis from RA and AS in the vast majority of cases."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
